```
{: .output}

Indeed, they are the `python` dependencies that are loaded before setting
the environment variables requiered to load `python`. Most modulefiles
automatically load their dependencies, but for some of them, we need to load
the dependencies manually.

To unload a specific module, e.g. `python`, run the command
`module unload python`. (On some systems, this will also unload the modules it
depends on.)

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module unload python
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module list
```
```
No modules loaded
```
{: .output}

If we wanted to unload everything at once (all modules), we could run
`module purge` (unloads everything).

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module load python 
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module purge
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module list
```
{: .language-bash}

```
No modules loaded
```
{: .output}
